EDI is an extensible line editor for the terminal and X11. It is
inspired by some of the IBM/DB2/ UI clones out there but rebuilt from
scratch in Lisp and ported to GNU/Linux environment.

This editor intends to have the following features:
  - Keyboard-driven control ontop of xkb/evdev bindings
  - Simplified modal editing interface, maps to Vi bindings by default
  - Colon commands ala StumpWM
  - S-expr-based config which can be inserted directly into our other
    core app configs in :EDITOR slot

and has intended use-cases:
  - drop-in line editor for lisp scripts
  - print/tail log files
  - interactive find/replace
  - trivial ediff

- why no emacs defaults?
  - My current global keymap is already saturated with emacs-style
    bindings and would cause conflicts when used from terminal
  - I like the idea of home-row-based control as a layer within an
    emacs-dominated environment
  - Modal editing is easier to create interesting abstractions for
    which we can use in other core apps